4. Analysis
My results show that K-d Tree is faster for larger datasets with a small number of dimensions.
For example, with 10,000 points in 2 dimensions, K-d Tree took 1.145 ms, while Brute Force took 47.152 ms.
However, when the number of dimensions increased, K-d Tree became slower.
With 10,000 points in 20 dimensions, K-d Tree took 890.177 ms, while Brute Force took 187.125 ms.
Brute Force checks every point, but its process is simple. In high dimensions, K-d Tree may also need to check many points. 
It also spends extra time searching branches and backtracking. Therefore, Brute Force can be faster.

5. Conclusion
K-d Tree works well for large datasets with low dimensions. Brute Force can be better for small datasets or high-dimensional data.
The best method depends on the number of points and the number of dimensions.
